# Citation File Format 1.2.0 — https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#     Every citation this project invites resolved to lapledger.org, which is
#     one domain registration. A reader who wants to cite the database — a
#     journalist, a Wikipedia editor arguing WP:RS, anyone filing a figure
#     they may have to defend later — had no citation to copy and nothing
#     naming the work apart from the page they were on. GitHub renders a
#     "Cite this repository" button from this file, in BibTeX and APA, at
#     zero maintenance.
#
#     `version` and `date-released` below are VERSION and BUILT in build.py.
#     They are not copied by hand and left to rot:
#     tests/test_conventions.py fails the build when they drift, the same way
#     a README figure that disagrees with the database does.
#
#     There is no `doi:` here. Nobody has minted one — Zenodo's GitHub
#     integration is an account switch a maintainer flips, not something a
#     build can do — and a DOI written down before it exists is a citation
#     that resolves to nothing. NULL means not established.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this database, please cite it as below.
title: "Lap Ledger: a verified SQLite database of Formula One"
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: Farley
    given-names: Alex
repository-code: https://github.com/Alex-Farley/formula-1-data
url: https://lapledger.org
abstract: >-
  A normalised SQLite database of Formula One — championships, races,
  finishing orders, drivers, constructors, circuits, chassis and engines —
  built from public sources by a dependency-free Python build and gated by
  cross-checks that refuse a fact they cannot reconcile. The database is a
  pure function of its sources: it rebuilds byte-for-byte, so a published
  digest identifies exactly one file. Where two sources disagree the
  difference is published rather than reconciled, and where nobody has
  established a fact it is recorded as a gap instead of being invented.
  Race timing is deliberately absent: no licence on offer permits passing
  it on.
keywords:
  - Formula One
  - motorsport
  - open data
  - SQLite
  - sports statistics
# Three licences, because the repository holds three kinds of thing. The code
# is MIT (LICENSE). The data is CC BY-SA 4.0 (LICENSE-DATA). The circuit
# centrelines traced from OpenStreetMap are ODbL 1.0 and ship as their own
# file, f1-geometry.db, so that share-alike follows that file alone and does
# not reach the rest. Dropping ODbL-1.0 from this list would state that the
# geometry is offered under the other two, which is the one thing the split
# exists to prevent.
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-SA-4.0
  - ODbL-1.0
version: "2.24"
date-released: "2026-09-16"
